# KNOWN GAP — the `Box[T](x)` CONSTRUCTOR ICEs on a closure while `Box.new(x)`
# compiles and runs. Two spellings of one operation, one of which panics.
#
#     Box[Callable[int(int)]] f = Box[Callable[int(int)]]((int n): n * 2)
#     print(f(21))
#         -> rc 101, panicked at src/ir/lowering/mod.rs:1724
#
#     Box[Callable[int(int)]] f = Box.new((int n): n * 2)
#     print(f(21))
#         -> rc 0, prints 42          <- CORRECT
#
# ⚠ `Box.new` is the spelling docs/book/16-smart-pointers.md:40,44 teaches, and
# it is the chapter's ONLY `Box.new` usage -- so the working path is the
# documented one and the ICE is on the ctor sibling.
#
# ⚠ MECHANISM: `src/ir/lowering/exprs/methods.rs:230-236` carries a closure
# carve-out that `calls.rs` lacks. That carve-out is itself a
# `starts_with("__Closure_")` NAME-MATCH -- deciding semantics from an
# identifier's spelling, which Layering rule 2 forbids. The reference-grade fix
# puts the flag on the typed decl, not the name.
#
# ⚠ This blocks "just mirror the self-host's single normalised Box path": the
# two Rust entry points are NOT equivalent today, and any unification must carry
# the carve-out or it regresses this to an ICE.
#
# ⊕ SEPARATE, FOUND ALONGSIDE: `(*f)` on a `Box[Callable[...]]` is rejected with
# `E_DerefNonBox: cannot dereference `*` a value of type `Box[Callable[int(int)]]`
# — `*` requires a `Box[T]``. The value IS a `Box[T]`; the diagnostic contradicts
# itself. Both spellings hit it, so it is independent of the ICE above.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 42, exactly as the `Box.new` spelling does.
# Found 2026-08-27 by R45 Track A brief-review passes 1-2; orchestrator-verified.

void main():
    Box[Callable[int(int)]] f = Box[Callable[int(int)]]((int n): n * 2)
    print(f(21))
